##### Story & continuity
- [ ] There is one protagonist (object / shape / mascot) visible or implied in every scene, and it ends inside the logo.
- [ ] No scene is "a frame whose content swaps on the beat" — that reads as PPT even inside one continuous camera.
- [ ] Every transition is listed in the shot map with its mechanism (match cut, morph, wipe, push, convergence, slam).
- [ ] The ending is born from the previous scene, not a separate end card.

##### Motion
- [ ] Each feature is shown *performing* (punch = impact, dots = rhythm, outline = draw-on, sticker = peel), never as a static after-image.
- [ ] Big moves land on analysed onsets; secondary moves on 8ths / 16ths; held sections breathe (slow drift, hang time).
- [ ] Animation principles: anticipation before hits, squash & stretch on landings, overshoot / spring settles, staggered follow-through, motion trails on fast objects.
- [ ] Two frame rates: hand-drawn lines boil at 12 fps, camera and objects stay at 60 fps.
- [ ] Easing has character: expo-out for UI and type, springs only for characters and stickers.

##### Type
- [ ] Text is part of the animation (written by the protagonist, rides a path, echoes an effect) — never a floating caption over it.
- [ ] No decorative / "art" fonts, no label chips, no lower-third templates, no boxes behind text.
- [ ] Handwriting fonts per language when the brand is doodle / indie (OFL: Patrick Hand EN/VI, ZCOOL KuaiLe ZH, Yomogi JA, Gaegu KO); one clean sans (SF Pro / PingFang) otherwise.
- [ ] Words are few; the picture carries the meaning.
- [ ] Nothing important is covered by particles / dots — clear a soft lane for the text, then let it dissolve back.

##### Material
- [ ] Assets look current (flash / film / candid, Gen-Z subjects) or are pure graphic; no dated stock, no code-drawn "ugly" mascots.
- [ ] Prefer half-body subjects, pets, objects; big close-up faces get covered by effects.
- [ ] Brand colours used as full-screen blocks at key hits; otherwise light, airy base with soft colour fields.
- [ ] The product's real design language (UI, icon, mascot) appears, but UI is a moment, not the whole film.

##### Languages
- [ ] One master cut (EN) + local cuts that swap only key lines; decorative multilingual text kept in all cuts.
- [ ] Never stack 5 languages in one block of text.
- [ ] Translations flagged for native-speaker review.

##### Delivery
- [ ] Audio runs full length in the MP4.
- [ ] Contact sheet checked; obvious bugs (off-screen elements, backwards text, seams, overlaps) fixed.
- [ ] File opened for the user + clickable links; side-by-side page for multiple cuts.


### Reference file: pro-motion/references/audio-first.md

#### Audio first: campaign brain → sound plan → prompt → post

The track is the film's skeleton and its most shareable asset. Think like a campaign first, let the sound go wild second, then lock the precise parts in code (`scripts/audio_post.py`). The shot map is written only after the track is locked.
Why sound leads: sonic devices gave 2.3× the brand fluency of a visual logo across 1,250+ Effie campaigns (System1); TikTok's 2-second sonic logo has 81% global recognition.

##### 1 · Campaign brain (answer in one line each before any music)

1. **Truth** — one thing the product really does that the film can *prove*, not claim. (Moldy Whopper: 34-day time-lapse proved "no preservatives". Hyundai "Night Fishing": shot only with the car's cameras.)
2. **Tension** — a complaint, rival, debate or ritual to flip. (Hyundai "Coquí Alarmed", Cannes 2026 Audio GP: a tourist asked how to silence Puerto Rico's coquí frogs → rental-car lock beep replaced by the frog; buyers asked for it, it became a permanent feature. Whopper Detour: 1¢ Whopper unlocked only within 600 ft of a McDonald's. Biti's Hunter: a #teamđi vs #teamtrởvề debate, answered with an MV.)
3. **Mechanic** — what people *do*, retellable in one sentence. (Field Barcode, Cannes 2026 Outdoor GP: the stadium grass mowed into a 104 m barcode, scannable from the TV broadcast for a discount. Uber Eats, Cannes 2026 Media GP: build your own Super Bowl ad from 1,000+ clips inside the app, each combination unlocks a deal. Budweiser "One Second Ads": guess the song from one beat.)
4. **Hijacked channel** — put the brand inside something people already look at or hear: grass stripes, a car's lock beep, a shutter sound, a photo-booth countdown, a station departure melody, map pins, game worlds, receipts, notification sounds. The medium is the idea.
5. **Audience as media** — they finish, remix, localise or star in it. (Mixue: public-domain melody, fan covers in 20+ languages. Spotify Wrapped. Shot on iPhone.)
6. **Moment** — attach to a ritual or peak (Tet, Super Bowl, a derby, exam season, payday) or a place (a stadium, a station, a booth).
7. **Afterlife** — the asset outlives the campaign: a sound in the product, a font, a feature (the coquí lock sound stayed).

Tests: retellable in one sentence · "inevitable once you see it" · would a journalist write about the mechanic without seeing the film? · would someone do it with no prize?

**Pick a mechanism type** (cases and principles in `campaign-cases.md`), then design the sound that *is* the mechanism:

| Type | Example | Sound that is the mechanism |
|---|---|---|
| A Physical binding (no action → no product) | Coca-Cola Friendly Twist, Milka neighbours | The track only completes with two people: two phones each play half (bass / melody) or one call + one answer; a hook that needs two voices |
| B Spatial impossibility | Skyworth "falling-out" TV, Nike shot across the river | Sound from the wrong place: a call at one location answered at another; a poster whose sound "escapes"; audio that only makes sense on site |
| C Shared moment / story | 啥是佩奇, Red Bull Stratos, Biti's | The sound of a moment everyone already feels (countdown, school bell, New Year), turned into the hook |
| D Co-creation | Spotify Wrapped, Uber Eats build-your-ad, Mixue covers | User material becomes the music: their photo → their own 5 s jingle; stems / swappable sections; crowd recorded by users |
| E Product fact → behaviour | Jif 1,300 texts, BK magnet wall, Budweiser 1-second | A countable fact becomes rhythm (N effects = N hits); tap / clap / shout the rhythm to unlock; 1-second guessable fragments |
| F Hijacked channel | Hyundai coquí lock beep, 999 dialogue billboards, Field Barcode | The sonic logo lives inside an existing sound: lock beep, shutter, booth countdown, notification, station melody; or "answers" another famous sound |
| G Imitable meme | Old Spice, Moldy Whopper, 嚼柠檬 | A 1–2 s hook anyone can copy with mouth, hands or body; absurd, quotable, parody-ready |

##### 2 · From campaign to sound (each answer becomes an audio decision)

| Brain answer | Becomes in the track |
|---|---|
| Truth | a sound made *by* the product or its action (shutter, punch, peel, scan beep) as the hook |
| Tension | contrast section: the complaint sound → flipped into the hook |
| Mechanic | a slot users fill: before/after glow-up, 4 photo slots on 4 hits, 1-second guessable fragments, a countdown |
| Channel | the sonic logo is designed to live there (lock beep, shutter, booth countdown, notification, station melody) — 1–3 s, recognisable alone |
| Audience | stems, a section that swaps per language/market, crowd recorded by real users |
| Moment | the cultural sound of that moment (countdown, cheer, chant) |
| Afterlife | motif short and clean enough to be the app's open / export sound |

##### 3 · Sound plan (the wild part, with rails)

**Fusion engine** — collide far-apart sources; each used as texture, never cliché:
- world instruments: gamelan, talking drum, Ethio-jazz brass, baile-funk tamborzão, tabla, Balkan brass, suona/pan-flute bursts, janggu, đàn bầu bends, chindon-ya, batucada;
- niche timbres: Omnichord, ondes Martenot, glass harmonica, Stroh violin, musical saw, kalimba, Mellotron, toy piano, music box;
- 2020s styles: live-drum new jazz, amapiano, 2020s J-pop band drive, Vietnamese folk-pop fusion, UK garage 2-step, Jersey club bounce, footwork, liquid DnB, ballroom vogue, digital cumbia, hip-hop breaks, R&B;
- found objects / foley as instruments: camera, sticker, tape, stamp, bubble wrap, gacha capsule, nail-polish shaker, receipt printer, flipbook paper, squeegee, letterpress, ink; nature: water drops, leaves, fire, lightning, blades; "feminine" synaesthesia: stars, moon hum, lake, pink = soda fizz + wind chimes.

**Sound-design toolbox** — hyperreal synthetic materials (SOPHIE: bubbles rise in pitch, metal clangs, latex stretches), found-sound chops (Fred again..), granular freeze/clouds, spectral resynthesis (Serum 2 imports PNGs), resonators tuned to the key fed by foley, physical-modelled mallets, FM / phase-distortion plucks, formant "talking" filters, stutter repeaters, extreme time-stretch, tape stops, reverse reverbs, dub throws. Mix: mono below 120 Hz, Haas 10–30 ms widening, M/S air on the sides, parallel-compressed drums, clear depth (dry close, huge space behind).

**Voice palette** — crowds: stadium, cheer squad, kids, fans, megaphone, "everyone in the world". Processing per appearance: octave double, ping-pong delay, pitched-up formant, distorted megaphone, slowed giant, granular freeze, vocoder, harmonizer stack + gated reverb, reverse-reverb swell. Brand name: a few key moments, shouted by a group, same two-note interval (sonic logo).

**Concept devices that worked** — "everyone joins" parade (Tutti = full ensemble) · sticker-book page flips (new world every 2 bars) · object drum kit (a girl's desk) · synaesthesia (element = colour = sound, one key, one reverb) · animation-studio sounds + "on twos" stutter · photo-booth countdowns in each market's language · Morse / spectrogram signatures. A purely random concept with no campaign mechanic was "boring".

**K-pop / TVC structure rails** — starts on the drop or hits by 3–5 s · beat switch every section · deliberate anti-drop or curveball once · chant slot · silence (½–1 beat) before the two biggest hits · surprise runs / fills between phrases (brass runs, harp glissandos, scratches, rewinds, pitch dives) · final peak *is* the logo · button ending · brand at 0 s and at the logo (bookend) · bars on round seconds (120 BPM → 2 s) so 6/15/full cut-downs splice cleanly · loopable end.

##### 4 · Prompt craft (Suno / Udio / ElevenLabs)

- Optional 1–2 sentence Background first (product, audience, what the music must do); cut it first when over the limit.
- Style field = identity (≤ ~1000 chars); section tags in the lyrics field = placement. Each instrument appears once.
- Per section describe energy, emotion and what's special; don't write chord progressions.
- Concrete instruments and gear beat genre labels; stacked electronic genre tags collapse into generic EDM.
- Write the arc: "starts at full energy, no intro… biggest peak at the end for the logo".
- Voices: describe the recording ("~20 young women shouting live in a room, chest voice, loose timing, breath before each shout, no auto-tune").
- `[Tape rip]` lines = arrangement cues; `(megaphone)` after a lyric = delivery. No artist names. Don't write "instrumental" if any voice is wanted.
- Seconds are hints. 3–4 takes, keep the right structure, fix timing in post.

##### 5 · Post (precise, in code)

```bash
python3 tools/analyze_music.py music/mono.wav music/                       # real bars and hits first
python3 tools/audio_post.py sfx bubble music/bubble.wav --note C5         # bubble punch metal rip click riser impact sparkle
python3 tools/audio_post.py lay   take.wav a.wav 4.0:music/bubble.wav 12.0:music/impact.wav:-2
python3 tools/audio_post.py phone a.wav b.wav --from 8 --to 11.5          # glow-up "before"
python3 tools/audio_post.py gate  b.wav c.wav --from 5.3 --to 8 --fps 24  # on twos
python3 tools/audio_post.py loop  c.wav d.wav
python3 tools/audio_post.py morse BRAND m.wav --bpm 120
python3 tools/audio_post.py stego d.wav logo.png e.wav --at 22 --dur 2
ffmpeg -i e.wav -af loudnorm=I=-14:TP=-1:LRA=11 track.wav                 # social; -23/-24 LUFS broadcast
```
Phone recordings of 10–20 real props beat synthesis for everyday objects and are licence-clean.

##### 6 · Rights

Original or public-domain melodies; no samples of released records; own or licensed foley; crowd voices with consent; check the generator's commercial terms. TikTok business accounts are limited to its Commercial Music Library (tightened July 2025) → an owned original track is an asset.

##### 7 · Taste notes (young-women photo app, CJKV + EN)

Rejected: soft brand intros, late drops, flat arrangements, generic EDM, too J-pop, fake AI vocals, whispers, "tu-tu-tu" stutters, over-repeated brand name, long cluttered prompts, random concepts without a mechanic.
Accepted: jazz fusion × glitch collage with a logo-scale climax; math rock × Shibuya-kei × bossa nova; global parade with crowd shouts; desk-object drum kit; glow-up slot + loop + localised cheers; photo-booth countdowns.


### Reference file: pro-motion/references/campaign-cases.md

#### Campaign case bank (mechanisms to borrow)

These campaigns don't persuade. They create an experience or event people can't ignore, so users become the media. Each entry: what · mechanism · why it worked. ✓ = checked against a source this session; unmarked = well-known or from the user's notes; (u) = figures not verified.

##### A · Physical mechanism: the product forces the action
- **Coca-Cola "Friendly Twist"** (2014, Colombia) — gear-shaped caps open only when two people twist them together; 3,000 bottles in campus vending machines in the first week of term. You can't drink without making a friend.
- **Milka "Last Square"** (2013, FR/DE) — 10M bars missing one square; a code lets you send that square to someone. The defect becomes a gift ritual. (u: +33% sales, Cannes Gold)
- **Milka "Let's Neighbor Up"** ✓ (Poland, Sept 2024, Ogilvy) — 100,000 parcels sent to the flat next door in new-build blocks; inside, two bars, one for the neighbour who brings it over. The "Neighborly" edition sold out: 5.5M buyers. Ran for three years (door hangers 2025, nameplates 2026).

##### B · Spatial impossibility: a scene that only exists on site
- **Skyworth wallpaper TV** ✓ (China, autumn 2026) — bus-shelter ads where the TV "slides out" of the frame: "Not the installer's fault — the TV is just too thin." Passers-by think the ad is broken; some brought a TV remote to pose.
- **Mizone protruding bottle** (2025) — half a 1.5 m bottle bursting out of bus-stop ads. Mixed reviews; the controversy itself spread.
- **Nike × Zheng Qinwen** (Shanghai, 2025) — poster on one bank of the Huangpu, a giant ball on the other: she "hit it across the river". The space between two places is the content.
- **Carlsberg "Probably the best poster"** (2015, London) — a billboard that pours free beer. The promise becomes a taste.
- **Mercado Livre "Field Barcode"** ✓ (Cannes 2026 Outdoor GP) — stadium grass mowed into a 104 m barcode, scannable from the TV broadcast for 25% off.

##### C · Story: borrow a moment everyone already feels
- **"What is Peppa?" (啥是佩奇)** (2019, China) — a rural grandpa builds a "Peppa" out of a blower for his grandson; Spring Festival guilt about not going home.
- **Red Bull Stratos** (2012) — a jump from ~39 km, live on YouTube; 8M+ concurrent viewers. The brand didn't claim energy; it staged a human limit.
- **Biti's Hunter "Đi để trở về"** ✓ (Vietnam, 2017–18) — an online #teamđi vs #teamtrởvề debate, answered with a Tet MV; reported sales +300% in a week (agency source).

##### D · Co-creation: the user is the hero
- **Spotify Wrapped** — your year as shareable cards: "this is me", not "Spotify is good".
- **Lay's "Do Us a Flavor"** — fans invent flavours for a prize; buyers become inventors.
- **Uber Eats "Build Your Own Super Bowl Commercial"** ✓ (Cannes 2026 Media GP) — remix 1,000+ unaired clips in the app; each combo unlocks a deal; best Super Bowl sales (+$36M).
- **Mixue jingle** ✓ — public-domain melody, fans made 20+ language covers on Bilibili.
- **Photo-booth collab frames** ✓ (Korea → Asia) — Photoism and Life4Cuts sell limited-time branded / idol frames in selected branches; fans travel to collect them.

##### E · Gamification: turn a product fact into a behaviour
- **Jif "Text PEANUT 1,300 times"** ✓ (Canada, Oct 2024, Leo Burnett Toronto) — a 1 kg jar holds 1,300+ peanuts; the first 1,300 people to text PEANUT 1,300 times got a free jar. Spam culture flipped.
- **Burger King magnet wall** ✓ (Singapore, Bugis MRT, June 2025) — a wall of fries-shaped magnets; each one is a voucher. Seeing and getting are the same act.
- **Kaufland "TURBOKADDY"** (Czechia, 2022) — a "grandparents' supermarket" builds a gamer shopping cart with an esports team. (u: +222% consideration)
- **Budweiser "One Second Ads"** ✓ (Cannes 2025 Audio GP) — guess the song from one beat.

##### F · Hijacked channel: the medium is the idea
- **Hyundai "Coquí Alarmed"** ✓ (Cannes 2026 Audio GP) — a tourist's complaint about Puerto Rico's frogs → rental cars' lock beep became the coquí call; buyers asked for it, it became a feature.
- **999 Cold Remedy "dialogue billboards"** ✓ (China, Jan 2026) — next to Jackson Yee's Huawei ad: "the handsome guy next door… wear more layers" (a meme about him refusing long johns). Also "blown crooked by the wind" boards in Beijing and an upside-down "666" board for the gaokao.
- **Whopper Detour** ✓ — the rival's restaurants unlock a 1¢ Whopper.

##### G · Imitable content: the ad is a meme
- **Old Spice "The Man Your Man Could Smell Like"** (2010) — absurd one-take, then hundreds of personalised video replies; endlessly parodied.
- **Dove "Real Beauty Sketches"** (2013) — two sketches of each woman, one from her own description and one from a stranger's; she discovers it herself.
- **Moldy Whopper** ✓ — a 34-day time-lapse as proof.
- **Yili "嚼柠檬" / Luckin × Duolingo "brand wedding"** ✓ (China, 2025) — absurd, meme-native plots with offline pop-ups.

##### Shared principles
1. **Mechanism > event.** It keeps working inside normal use (a cap, Wrapped, a broken-looking ad).
2. **Bind the action to the core need.** Not "encourage sharing": no friend, no Coke; no peeling, no fries.
3. **User is the hero, brand is the tool.**
4. **Embed in an existing moment or emotion** (first week of term, Tet, waiting for the bus, a cold snap, a celebrity meme).
5. **Physical impossibility is the scarcest content.** It can't be seen online, so people have to go and film it.
6. **Proof over claim; channel is the idea; plan the afterlife.**


### Reference file: pro-motion/references/lessons.md

#### Lessons (bugs and process mistakes already paid for)

##### Rendering
- **ffmpeg `-shortest` with a slow image pipe truncated the MP3 audio at ~13 s** (v2). Render frames to JPEGs first, then mux in one pass with `-map 0:v -map 1:a -t <dur>`; never `-shortest`. `render-fast.js` does this. Always check the audio duration of the output.
- Single-tab serial rendering + `-preset slow` took ~5 min per 25 s. Parallel tabs (6) + `-preset veryfast -crf 19` takes ~30 s for 20 s @60 fps.
- Parallel tabs mean `render(t)` must be deterministic: `Math.random()` in a noise texture made every worker different → flicker. Use a seeded generator.
- Puppeteer's bundled Chrome download can hang; install with `PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD=1` and point at the system Chrome. Killing an in-progress `npm i` rolls back node_modules.
- `ffmpeg-static` binary may land without the exec bit: `chmod +x`.
- Canvas `getImageData` on images needs http, not file:// — the renderers serve the folder over a local http server.

##### Geometry & drawing
- Vision face boxes are normalized (0–1, top-left origin after flipping). Passing them into a pixel-space helper put the orbit ring and CCTV box off-screen. Convert explicitly.
- A rect + arc path clipped with `evenodd` drew a thin diagonal seam (implicit connecting line). `moveTo(cx + r, cy)` before `arc`, close the path.
- Text placed along an ellipse reads backwards on the near half unless the angle runs the other way; the far half being mirrored is physically correct.
- Spread ring text with `step = 2π / chars.length` or a gap rotates through the front.
- A full-bleed close-up leaves no room for clones / echoes; pull back to a graphic layout first.
- Fonts must be awaited (`document.fonts.load`) for every family used, including handwriting fonts, before the first frame.

##### Design process
- Designing from memory produced templates. Pulling real frames (contact sheets) of references fixed taste fast.
- Stills-only checks missed motion/sync problems and the audio bug; always check audio length and a frame sheet of the final file.
- Code-drawn mascots and dated stock photos made everything look cheap ("素材丑 → 成片一定丑").
- Content swapping in a fixed frame = PPT, even with a continuous camera.
- Labels as lower-third templates, chips and boxes were rejected; text must be animated into the scene.
- Multiple languages in one block looked messy; master + per-language cuts was accepted.
- The user doesn't want repeated verification rounds; do one competent check pass, fix, deliver.

##### Style (from a stylized brand film)
- The brief said "strongly stylized", not "pop art"; I collapsed four rich references (Persona 5, Promare, Mind Game, P5 anime) into the most obvious one (Lichtenstein pop art) because it was the first association and the easiest to draw in code. The output was good but generic relative to the references.
- Fix: technique sheets per reference + 3 divergent style frames for the user to choose before building (SKILL.md §2b). Name the techniques you are borrowing; if a direction can be summarised as one familiar label ("pop art", "flat", "Y2K"), it is probably not new enough.
- Techniques that were seen but not used, and were the distinctive part: Promare's flat polygon light and neon fields with zero gradients; Mind Game's mid-shot switches between drawing styles and photo collage; P5 anime's repeated framing (same train window, changing passengers); P5's silhouette crowds and graphic city maps.

##### Music (audio-first, from a launch where the track was made from scratch)
- Starting the picture before the track was locked wasted rounds; the user calls audio first "the most important". Plan the music as a campaign asset first (`references/audio-first.md`).
- Prompts listing many electronic genre tags came back as generic EDM; prompts naming concrete acoustic instruments over a live-player foundation came back tasteful.
- Generators ignore precise seconds and default to slow intros; state "starts at full energy, no intro" and "biggest peak at the end for the logo", then fix timing in post.
- AI crowd vocals sound fake unless the recording situation is described; whispers and stuttered brand names were rejected; the brand name works best shouted by a group at a few key moments.
- Random / generative concepts without a marketing mechanism were called "boring"; every concept needs a reason to be shared.


### Reference file: pro-motion/references/research-notes.md

#### What to take from each reference

##### Spotify Wrapped 2025 (60fps.design screen recordings; Spotify design Q&A)
- Patterns (stripes, polka dots, concentric rings, wave bands) ARE the transitions; they scale up to fill the frame and hug content edges.
- One thin hand-drawn line threads screen to screen, forming stars, circling numbers.
- Extreme type scale contrast: huge stretched display numerals vs tiny clean text.
- Photos framed by pattern borders (stamp edges, stripe frames) become graphic objects.
- Black / off-white base, one accent colour per moment; full-pattern screens alternate with airy ones.

##### Figma Config openers (Relay, Copenhagen; Figma brand blog)
- 2025: frame rate dropped 60 → 15 fps for a tactile, handmade feel.
- 2026: glyphs and textures thread, stack, loop and break out of containers — "looser, more collage-y".
- Static design and motion developed together; dedicated sound design.

##### Ordinary Folk — Gemini app launch (2D UI MG)
- Soft pastel gradient fields, one centred line with a single highlighted word, UI crops, floating phones, dark section for contrast, glowing ring for the climax.

##### OpenAI — ChatGPT Pulse launch
- White canvas, small centred type, huge whitespace, a plain white phone, beautiful illustrations as the hero, UI chips animating in.

##### Linear / Cursor / Raycast (Impractical breakdowns)
- Masked text reveals from invisible edges (no blur/scale fades); sharp acceleration + long deceleration, no bounce; generous holds.
- Hard cuts are fine when framing changes; aggressive punch-ins on the moment of value.
- Raycast: withhold information, scramble text, audio drop-outs, a zero-ease punch-in finale.

##### BUCK × Notion AI; Duolingo (Rive)
- Hand-drawn characters animated in cel for handmade feel; personality from the timing of a blink.
- Story over feature list; every cut on an audio beat; type integrated with character motion.

##### Category / competitor App Store art (photo-trend apps)
- dooo0t: Y2K glitter, scribbled stars/hearts, doodle outline around people, stamp-edge frames.
- WalkColor: italic serif + highlighter blocks, dot-grid paper, half-split punch-hole swaps, offset colour blocks behind tilted phones.
- DotArt: bold half-and-half compositions (stripes + notes, polka holes revealing a face).
- Their photos: flash, film, candid Gen-Z subjects = "网感".

##### Code-motion effect libraries (ideas, re-implemented in canvas)
- React Bits: SplitFlapText, RotatingText, CircularText / CurvedLoop, StickerPeel, HalftoneReveal, DotGrid, MetaBalls, CircularGallery / DomeGallery, FlyingPosters, PixelTransition, ImageTrail, Shuffle.

##### Getting frames when browsing is blocked
- The in-app browser may be refused on YouTube / Vimeo and trigger per-site permission prompts; use `scripts/ref_frames.sh` (yt-dlp + ffmpeg) instead. Behance blocks curl; 60fps.design pages expose direct mp4 URLs.


### Reference file: pro-motion/references/sourcing.md

#### Finding references (what actually worked)

##### Pipeline
1. **Find the official upload** with web search restricted to youtube.com:
   `youtube <title> official trailer OR opening OR "textless OP"` — textless OP/ED versions are best (no credits over the art).
2. **Download low-res and tile 24 frames** (no browser needed, no permission prompts):
   `bash tools/ref_frames.sh <url> <name> 24` → `<name>_sheet.jpg` (6×4 across the whole clip). Look at it with the image viewer.
3. For a moment you care about, grab dense frames: `$FF -ss 31 -t 3 -i name.mp4 -vf "fps=8,scale=-2:300,tile=8x3" -frames:v 1 dense.jpg` — transitions and timing only show up at 8+ fps.
4. Motion-design / UI motion: 60fps.design pages contain direct mp4 links (ref_frames.sh handles them). Impractical (impractical.ai/launch-videos) has written breakdowns.
5. Category look: App Store screenshots of competitors; Behance project images can be listed from an already-open browser tab via `javascript_tool` (curl is blocked), then downloaded from the CDN.

##### Blocked / painful
- The in-app browser asks the user to allow every new site and YouTube may be refused → prefer yt-dlp in the shell.
- Vimeo now requires login for yt-dlp; look for a YouTube mirror.
- Behance/YouTube pages block curl; their CDNs (mir-s3-cdn-cf.behance.net, i.ytimg.com) don't.

##### A wider pool for "strongly stylized" (pick across countries and media)
- **Japan:** Persona 5 opening (limited red/black/white, halftone, shard cuts, silhouettes, repeated framing), Promare / Trigger (flat polygon light, neon fields, no gradients), Masaaki Yuasa — Mind Game, Devilman Crybaby, Inu-Oh (elastic bodies, style switching, photo collage), Studio 4°C — Tekkonkinkreet (painted density), Mob Psycho 100 (painted-on-glass effects), Kill la Kill (type as staging).
- **US:** Spider-Verse films (halftone on 3D, misregistration, on-twos characters, comic captions), The Mitchells vs the Machines (doodle overlays on 3D), Puss in Boots: The Last Wish (painterly fight smears), Arcane (painted textures, ink flashes), Saul Bass titles, Catch Me If You Can titles (Kuntzel + Deygas cut-paper).
- **Europe:** Gobelins graduation shorts, Cartoon Saloon (Wolfwalkers: visible construction lines, woodcut textures), Klaus (2D with volumetric light), Yellow Submarine (psychedelic flat).
- **China:** 雾山五行 (ink action, brush speed), 中国奇谭 (style anthology: ink, paper-cut, stop motion).
