## SKILL: design-token-contrast ⟦S0248⟧

**When to use:** Checks WCAG 2.2 contrast between the color tokens of a project (light and dark theme) and suggests the closest passing color that keeps the hue.

### Design Token Contrast

#### Overview

Measures contrast on the **tokens**, not on screens: a problem fixed in a token is fixed
everywhere it is used. A Node checker, shipped as source in
[references/contraste-checker.md](references/contraste-checker.md), reads the CSS custom properties
of the project, pairs text tokens with background tokens, measures each pair in the light theme
and in dark mode, and, for every pair that fails, proposes the color with the same hue and the
nearest lightness that passes. It also proposes **one value per token** that passes against every
background it is measured on, so the fix is a single change.

WCAG 2.2 AA minimums it applies: normal text **4.5:1** (1.4.3), large text **3:1**, and UI
components and graphics such as input borders, icons and focus rings **3:1** (1.4.11).

#### When to Use This Skill

- Use when the palette changes, a color is added, or a light or dark theme is about to be approved.
- Use when the user asks whether a text color is readable on its background.
- Use when an accessibility audit flags contrast and you want the fix at the token level.

#### How It Works

##### Step 1: Run the measurement

The checker ships as source, not as a committed script: save the JavaScript block in
[references/contraste-checker.md](references/contraste-checker.md) as `contraste.mjs` in a
scratch directory (no dependencies, Node 18+), then run it:

```bash
node contraste.mjs --tokens src/styles/tokens.css
```

Without `--tokens` it looks for `src/styles/tokens.css` and other common locations. The script:

- Reads `--color-*` variables in hex, `rgb()`, `hsl()` or `oklch()`, resolves `var(--other)`, and
  separates the default theme from dark mode (`prefers-color-scheme: dark`, `.dark` or
  `[data-theme=dark]`).
- Pairs tokens by name: text tokens (`text`, `foreground`, `link`, `focus`…) over background tokens
  (`background`, `bg`, `surface`, `card`, `canvas`…). Spanish token names (`texto`, `fondo`,
  `superficie`, `tarjeta`) are recognized too.
- Prints `OK` or `NO` per pair, with the ratio, the minimum and the suggestion.

If token names do not follow that pattern, write the real pairs to a JSON file and pass it with
`--pares` (the keys are `texto`, `fondo` and `tipo`, where `tipo` is `texto` or `ui`):

```json
[{"texto": "--color-gray-500", "fondo": "--color-white", "tipo": "texto"},
 {"texto": "--color-input-border", "fondo": "--color-white", "tipo": "ui"}]
```

To explore a new palette, `--todos` measures every color against every other. `--json out.json`
writes the results for further processing. The script's output is in Spanish; ratios and token
names read the same in any language.

##### Step 2: Check what the script cannot see

- Text over **images or gradients**: capture the section and check the worst spot.
- Text with **opacity**, or colors hard-coded outside the tokens (search for `#[0-9a-fA-F]{3,8}`,
  `rgb(`, `text-[#`).
- States: `hover`, `disabled` (exempt, but still distinguishable), and placeholders that carry
  information.

##### Step 3: Report and propose

| Pair | Now | Minimum | Suggestion | Where it is used |
|---|---|---|---|---|

Search for the classes and `var(--color-...)` references to fill "where it is used".

#### Examples

##### Example 1: A gray that fails on white

```text
== tema claro (por defecto): 4 colores leídos ==
  NO  3.54:1 (mín. 4.5) --color-text #888888 sobre --color-background #ffffff
       sugerencia para --color-text: oklch(56.2% 0.000 89.9) (#757575) → 4.62:1
```

Propose `#767676` for `--color-text` (the single value that passes on all its backgrounds), apply
it only after the user agrees, and run the script again to show it passes.

#### Best Practices

- ✅ Fix the token once instead of patching each screen.
- ✅ Offer options: darken the token, keep the color only for large text, or add a new token
  (`--color-accent-text`) for small text and keep the original for fills and borders.
- ❌ Don't change brand colors without asking.
- ❌ Don't report a theme as passing without checking dark mode too.

#### Limitations

- It measures tokens, not rendered pixels: images, gradients and opacity need a visual check.
- Pairing by name is a heuristic; use `--pares` when names are unconventional.
- This skill does not replace environment-specific validation, testing, or expert review.

#### Security & Safety Notes

- The script only reads the CSS or JSON file you point it to and writes a file only with `--json`.
  It makes no network requests and installs nothing. Requires Node 18+.
- It never edits tokens by itself; changes are proposed and applied after the user approves.

#### Common Pitfalls

- **Problem:** A dark-mode pair shows `OK` against the light background.
  **Solution:** Make sure dark-mode variables live in a `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, `.dark` or
  `[data-theme=dark]` block so the script can separate the themes.

#### Related Skills

- `@wcag-audit-patterns` - For a full WCAG 2.2 audit beyond color contrast.

#### Parser and suggestion limits

This checker uses regular expressions rather than the browser CSS cascade: scoped selectors, specificity, nested rules, layers, imports and computed values can be misinterpreted. Review explicit pairs against computed rendered styles. A single suggestion is sought within each measured theme, not guaranteed across all themes or backgrounds. Lightness is searched on a discrete grid while chroma may be reduced; neither exact nearest color nor unchanged hue after sRGB clipping/rounding is guaranteed. Re-measure the exact serialized color before applying it. Transparent backgrounds need their actual underlying composite; the checker refuses those pairs rather than assuming white.

Unparsed tokens make coverage incomplete. The lightweight color parser is not a CSS validator and cannot certify color syntax or gamut mapping. Focus requirements also depend on state/context and other WCAG criteria. Read the source before running it on approved inputs; report output uses exclusive private file creation and will fail if the destination exists. No checker or rendered-page validation was executed during this review.


### Reference file: design-token-contrast/references/contraste-checker.md

#### Checker source

`design-token-contrast` ships its checker as source so the review stays auditable and
dependency-free. Save the block below as `contraste.mjs` somewhere you can run Node 18+
from, then invoke it with `node contraste.mjs`. No dependencies.

```javascript
#!/usr/bin/env node
// Calcula el contraste WCAG 2.x entre los colores de tus tokens y sugiere un ajuste cuando un par
// no llega al mínimo. Lee variables CSS (--color-*) en hex, rgb(), hsl() u oklch(), y resuelve
// alias var(--otro-token). No necesita dependencias.
//
// Uso:
//   node contraste.mjs                               # busca src/styles/tokens.css
//   node contraste.mjs --tokens ruta/tokens.css
//   node contraste.mjs --pares .kit/contraste.json   # pares explícitos
//   node contraste.mjs --todos                       # matriz con todas las combinaciones
//   node contraste.mjs --json informe.json
//
// Formato de --pares: [{"texto": "--color-texto", "fondo": "--color-fondo", "tipo": "texto"}]
//   tipo: "texto" (4,5:1), "texto-grande" (3:1, desde 24 px o 18,66 px en negrita), "ui" (3:1,
//   bordes de campos, íconos, indicador de foco: criterio 1.4.11).
// Sale con código 1 si algún par falla, y con código 2 si no pudo medir nada o algún par quedó sin
// medir (un token que no existe o no es un color): un informe incompleto nunca dice «todo cumple».

import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';

const argv = process.argv.slice(2);
const op = { tokens: null, pares: null, todos: false, json: null };
for (let i = 0; i < argv.length; i++) {
  const a = argv[i];
  if (a === '--tokens') op.tokens = argv[++i];
  else if (a === '--pares') op.pares = argv[++i];
  else if (a === '--todos') op.todos = true;
  else if (a === '--json') op.json = argv[++i];
  else if (a === '--ayuda' || a === '-h') { console.log('Uso: node contraste.mjs [--tokens archivo.css] [--pares pares.json] [--todos] [--json salida.json]'); process.exit(0); }
  else { console.error(`Opción desconocida: ${a}`); process.exit(2); }
  if (['--tokens', '--pares', '--json'].includes(a) && (!argv[i] || argv[i].startsWith('--'))) { console.error(`Falta valor: ${a}`); process.exit(2); }
}

function buscarTokens() {
  const candidatos = ['src/styles/tokens.css', 'src/styles/global.css', 'src/app/globals.css', 'app/globals.css', 'src/index.css', 'styles/tokens.css'];
  for (const c of candidatos) if (fs.existsSync(c)) return c;
  return null;
}
const archivo = op.tokens || buscarTokens();
if (!archivo || !fs.existsSync(archivo)) {
  console.error('No encontré el archivo de tokens. Indícalo con --tokens ruta/al/archivo.css');
  process.exit(2);
}
const css = fs.readFileSync(archivo, 'utf8');

// ---------- Lectura de variables por bloque ----------
// Separamos las variables del tema claro (por defecto) de las de modo oscuro.
const DECLARACION = /(--[\w-]+)\s*:\s*([^;{}]+)/g;
// Un selector es de modo oscuro si nombra .dark o [data-theme="dark"] en cualquier posición
// (.dark, .dark:root, :root.dark, html[data-theme=dark]), salvo dentro de :not(...).
const esSelectorOscuro = (sel) => /\[data-theme=["']?dark["']?\]|\.dark(?![\w-])/.test(sel.replace(/:not\([^)]*\)/g, ''));
function leerVariables(texto) {
  const claro = new Map();
  const oscuro = new Map();
  let resto = texto.replace(/\/\*[\s\S]*?\*\//g, '');
  // 1) @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { ... } completo, con todos sus bloques internos.
  for (const m of resto.matchAll(/@media[^{]*prefers-color-scheme:\s*dark[^{]*\{((?:[^{}]*\{[^{}]*\})*[^{}]*)\}/g)) {
    for (const v of m[1].matchAll(DECLARACION)) oscuro.set(v[1], v[2].trim());
    resto = resto.replace(m[0], '');
  }
  // 2) Bloques cuyo selector es de modo oscuro.
  for (const m of resto.matchAll(/([^{}]*)\{([^{}]*)\}/g)) {
    if (!esSelectorOscuro(m[1])) continue;
    for (const v of m[2].matchAll(DECLARACION)) oscuro.set(v[1], v[2].trim());
    resto = resto.replace(m[0], '');
  }
  for (const v of resto.matchAll(DECLARACION)) claro.set(v[1], v[2].trim());
  return { claro, oscuro };
